{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|nys|kwaka}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkwɒkə/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒkə|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[cat]]-sized [[marsupial]], {{taxfmt|Setonix brachyurus|species}}, of southwestern [[Australia]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=John Long|title=Introduced Mammals of the World: Their History, Distribution and Influence|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=7YC3cYhGMOcC&pg=PA29&dq=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=5-AUUtWkF8WhiAe8rYGACQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&f=false|page=29
|passage=At least 673 '''quokkas''' were re-introduced from Rottnest Island to the Marsupial Research Station of the University of Western Australia (254 ha) at Jandakot from 1972 to 1988.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Hugh Tyndale-Biscoe|title=Life of Marsupials|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=gDFeNMhJlPUC&pg=PA354&dq=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=5-AUUtWkF8WhiAe8rYGACQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&f=false|page=354
|passage=Tammars, '''quokkas''' and black-footed rock wallabies inhabit islands in the southwest, while three species of hare wallaby, the burrowing bettong and five species of rock wallaby inhabit various islands in the northwest.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Ken Richardson|title=Australia's Amazing Kangaroos: Their Conservation, Unique Biology and Coexisternce with Humans|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=oVXRDFpvfkkC&pg=PA125&dq=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=5-AUUtWkF8WhiAe8rYGACQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22quokka%22%7C%22quokkas%22&f=false|page=125
|passage=Older unburnt areas (more than 25 years) on their own appear unable to sustain a '''quokka''' population.<br>The largest number of '''quokkas''' occurs on Rottnest Island near Perth, where the population estimates vary from 8000 to 12,000 individuals.}}

